    $1$ & Brokett问题 & $2$ & 多项式根的分布 &
    $3$ & HUM方法 & $4$ & 热方程波方程能控性\\
    $5$ & 自适应控制 & $6$ & $H^\infty$控制Robust控制 &
    $7$ & 滤波理论 & $8$ & 平均场理论\\
    $9$ & 松弛控制 & $10$ & 期权定价 &
    $11$ & Backstep方法 & $12$ & 大道性质\\
    $13$ & 有限时间能稳 & $14$ & 时间不一致问题 &
    $15$ & 风险扭曲 & $16$ & 强化学习与最优控制\\
    $17$ & 爆破问题
\end{tabular}\end{center}
We give here an example of how the \textbf{Hilbert Uniqueness Method} works.

The major reference is \cite{L1}, and the original paper is \cite{L2}.

=== $\boxed{1}$ Problem ===

Consider the wave equation
\[
    \left\{\begin{array}{l}
        y''-\triangle y=0 &(x,t)\in\Omega\times(0,T)\\
        y(0)=y^0,y'(0)=y^1 &\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!x\in\Omega\\
        y=\begin{cases}v\\0\end{cases} &\qquad\ \ 
        \begin{aligned}
            (x,t)&\in\Sigma\\
            (x,t)&\in(\partial\Omega\times(0,T))\backslash\Sigma
        \end{aligned}
    \end{array}\right..
\]
Here
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\Omega$ is an open set of $\R^n$, with $C^2$ boundary.
    \item $\Sigma$ is a nonempty open subset of $\partial\Omega\times(0,T)$.
    \item $'$ denotes $\dfrac{\partial}{\partial t}$, and the second line means 
    \[y(x,0)=y^0(x), \ \frac{\partial y}{\partial t}(x,0)=y^1(x),\quad x\in\Omega.\]
    \item the \textbf{initial data} $\{y^0,y^1\}$, 
    which will be chosen in some Hilbert space, is given.
    \item the \textbf{control function} $v$, 
    in some suitable Hilbert space also yet to be determined, 
    acts on the system via part of the boundary, namely $\Sigma$, 
    and is up to our choice.
\end{itemize}

Our goal is:
\begin{problem}
    Given $T>0$ and $\{y^0,y^1\}$ in an appropriate space, 
    find a control $v$ on $\Sigma$, such that
    \[y(T)=0,\ y'(T)=0,\quad x\in\Omega.\]
\end{problem}
Again, the expression above means
\[y(x,T)=0, \ \frac{\partial y}{\partial t}(x,T)=0,\quad x\in\Omega.\]
What concerns us are
\begin{itemize}
    \item the time $T>0$ when the system arrives at the balanced state.
    \item the space that $\{y^0,y^1\}$ lies in.
    \item the space that $v$ lies in.
\end{itemize}

=== $\boxed{2}$ Solution ===

We take the following steps.
\begin{itemize}
\item
(\textit{Preparation: $\phi$ with properties of use}) Start with the wave equation
\begin{equation}\label{1}
    \left\{\begin{array}{l}
        \phi''-\triangle \phi=0, &\!\!(x,t)\in\Omega\times(0,T)\\
        \phi(0)=\phi^0,\phi'(0)=\phi^1 &\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!x\in\Omega\\
        \phi=0, &\ (x,t)\in\partial\Omega\times(0,T)
    \end{array}\right.,
\end{equation}
which has a unique solution. Three proofs to such result 
are provided in \cite{L1} Chapter 1 section 3.2: 
separation of variables, Hille--Yosida, and Lax--Milgram.

\item (\textit{The \textcolor{ff3333}{``Inverse''} Problem}) Then solve
\begin{equation}\label{2}
    \left\{\begin{array}{l}
        \psi''-\triangle \psi=0, &\!\!(x,t)\in\Omega\times(0,T)\\
        \psi(T)=0,\psi'(T)=0 &\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!x\in\Omega\\
        \psi=\begin{cases}\dfrac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}\\0\end{cases} &\qquad\ \ 
        \begin{aligned}
            (x,t)&\in\Sigma\\
            (x,t)&\in(\partial\Omega\times(0,T))\backslash\Sigma
        \end{aligned}\end{array}\right.,
\end{equation}
where $\phi$ is from (\ref{1}), 
and $\nu$ is the unit outward normal vector of $\Omega$. 
Be aware that $\psi$ is simply another name for $y$ of the problem.

This is a non-homogeneous boundary value problem, 
which also admits a weak solution $\psi$. 
In terms of this result, \cite{L2} directs us to \cite{LM}.
\item (\textit{$\Lambda$: from (\ref{1}) to (\ref{2})}) 
We therefore define a linear operator
\begin{align*}
    \Lambda\colon & C_c^\infty(\Omega) \times C_c^\infty(\Omega) \to E,\\
    & \{\phi^0,\phi^1\} \mapsto \{\psi'(0),-\psi(0)\},
\end{align*}
where the space $E$ depends on what we know about 
the regularity of $\{\psi'(0),-\psi(0)\}$ 
(cf. \cite{L1} Chapter 1 section 4.2).
\item (\textit{Calculations}) Compute the bilinear form below, 
where $\langle-,-\rangle$ is the $L^2(\Omega) \times L^2(\Omega)$ inner product.
\begin{equation}\label{3}\begin{aligned}
    \langle\Lambda\{\phi^0,\phi^1\},\{\phi^0,\phi^1\}\rangle
    &=\langle\{\psi'(0),-\psi(0)\},\{\phi(0),\phi'(0)\}\rangle\\
    &=\int_\Omega \psi'(0)\phi(0)-\psi(0)\phi'(0)\\
    &=\int_\Sigma \bigg(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}\bigg)^2.
\end{aligned}\end{equation}
\begin{proofc}[How to get the last equality]

    Applying Green's formula in the space (not space--time), we get
    \[
        \int_{\Omega\times(0,T)} \psi\triangle\phi-\phi\triangle\psi
        =\int_{\partial\Omega\times(0,T)} \psi\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}
        -\phi\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\nu}
        =\int_{\partial\Omega\times(0,T)} \psi\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}
        =\int_\Sigma \bigg(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}\bigg)^2.\quad
    \]
    Plus, one--dimensional calculus on the $t$-axis yields
    \[\int_{\Omega\times(0,T)}(\psi\phi''-\phi\psi'')
    =\int_{\Omega\times T}(\psi\phi'-\phi\psi')
    -\int_{\Omega\times 0}(\psi\phi'-\phi\psi')
    =\int_{\Omega\times0}(\phi\psi'-\psi\phi').\]
    The left sides of the two lines coincide by the wave equation, 
    thus so do the right sides.
\end{proofc}
\item \textcolor{ff00ff}{(\textit{Key step: A Supposition})} 
The expression above is a \textbf{seminorm} 
on $C_c^\infty(\Omega) \times C_c^\infty(\Omega)$, 
and that it is a \textbf{norm} is equivalent to the following uniqueness theorem 
(though at the moment we do not know whether or not it is true).
\begin{UT}
    For $\{\phi^0,\phi^1\}\in C_c^\infty(\Omega) \times C_c^\infty(\Omega)$, 
    if the solution $\phi$ to (\ref{1}) satisfies
    \[\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}=0\quad\text{on }\Sigma,\]
    then $\phi=0$ in $\Omega\times(0,T)$.
\end{UT}
$\boxed{\text{One not really interested in mathematical strictness 
may now simply move on to the final step.}}$
\item (\textit{Completion of Initial Space}) 
Suppose that the uniqueness theorem holds, then (\ref{3}) defines 
a norm on $C_c^\infty(\Omega) \times C_c^\infty(\Omega)$, written $\|\ \ \|_F$. 
Let the \textcolor{cc9999}{\textbf{Hilbert space}} $F$ be the completion of 
$C_c^\infty(\Omega) \times C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ with respect to $\|\ \ \|_F$. 
(Note that the construction of $F$ guarantees that 
$\dfrac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}$ is always in $L^2(\Sigma)$.)
\item ($\Lambda$: \textit{invertible now}) 
Then extend $\Lambda$ to be a linear operator
\[\Lambda\colon F\to F'\]
through the diagram below, where $F'$ is the dual space of $F$ 
with respect to the $L^2(\Omega) \times L^2(\Omega)$ inner product, that is, 
the space consisting of bounded linear funtionals on $F$ 
identified with functions (distributions) on $\Omega$ via the pairing 
between $F'$ and $F$ (i.e. $\langle-\,,-\rangle$ in the diagram) 
that extends the $L^2(\Omega) \times L^2(\Omega)$ inner product 
on $C_c^\infty(\Omega) \times C_c^\infty(\Omega)$.
\[\langle\Lambda\{\phi^0,\phi^1\},\{\zeta^0,\zeta^1\}\rangle
=\langle\{\phi^0,\phi^1\},\{\zeta^0,\zeta^1\}\rangle_F.\]
\begin{cd}
    \qquad\, F\times F \ar[drr,"\langle-\,,-\rangle_F"] 
    \ar[dd,"\textcolor{red}{\Lambda}"description,dashed] & & \\
    & & \R\\
    \qquad \textcolor{red}{F'}\times F \ar[urr,"\langle-\,,-\rangle"']  & & 
\end{cd}
Conversely, by the Riesz representation theorem, 
each funtional $f\in F'$ (actually $f=\Lambda\{\phi^0,\phi^1\}$) 
induces exactly one $\{\phi^0,\phi^1\}\in F$. 
That is to say, $\Lambda$ is an invertible operator.
\item (\textit{$\Lambda^{-1}$: from (\ref{2}) to (\ref{1})}) 
$\Lambda$ being invertible permits us to find, 
as long as the initial data $\{y^1,-y^0\}\in F'$, the unique pair 
$\{\phi^0,\phi^1\}\in F$ such that
\[\Lambda\{\phi^0,\phi^1\}=\{y^1,-y^0\}.\]

\item (\textit{The Final Strike: finding $v$}) Let
\[v=\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu} \in L^2(\Sigma).\]
\end{itemize}

=== $\boxed{3}$ Remark ===

In summary, with a specific control 
$\dfrac{\partial\phi}{\partial\nu}$ on $\Sigma$, 
we get the initial state that will evolve into the balanced state at $T$; 
when given an initial state in an attempt to 
find a control forcing it into the balanced state at $T$, 
the control is obtained by reversing the process. 
Or in other words, operating $\Lambda$ is what we have already been able to do, 
and $\Lambda^{-1}$ solves the problem.

The fact that $\Lambda$ is invertible is a consequence of 
introducing the Hilbert structure $F$ on the space of initial data. 
The difficulty, then, lies in establishing the uniqueness theorem. 
Finally, two concluding remarks are in order.

\begin{itemize}
    \item[Q] Does the time $T$ play any role in the problem?
    \item[A] Yes. Recall that as a hyperbolic system, the effect of 
    boundary data (here the control $v$) is of finite propagation speed. 
    So $T$ has to be taken \textbf{sufficiently large}. 
    ($T$ is not big enough in the picture, as the shaded cone of dependence 
    at $(x^0,T)$ does not touch $\partial\Omega\times(0,T)$.)
    \begin{tikzpicture}
    \draw (0,1.5) ellipse (3 and 1);
    \draw (3,0) arc (0:-180:3 and 1);
    \draw[dashed] (3,0) arc (0:180:3 and 1);
    \draw (3,0) -- (3,1.5);
    \draw (-3,0) -- (-3,1.5);
    \filldraw[lightgray,fill opacity=0.5,line width=0.1] 
    (2,0) arc (0:-180:1.5 and 0.5) -- (-1,0) -- (0.5,1.5) -- (2,0);
    \node (T) at (-3.3,1.5) {$T$};
    \node (0) at (-3.3,0) {$0$};
    \node (O) at (-2,0) {$\Omega$};
    \node (xT) at (0.5,1.6) {$(x^0,T)$};
    \node (P) at (2.15,0.3) {\rotatebox{20}{$\partial\Omega\times(0,T)$}};
    \end{tikzpicture}
    
    \item[Q] Are the spaces $F,F'$ necessarily abstract?
    \item[A] Surely not. In some cases $F$ can be characterized explicitly.
    For example, if 
    \[T>2\sup_{x\in\Omega}|x-x^0|,\quad 
    \Sigma=\{x\in\partial\Omega\mid(x-x^0)\cdot\nu(x)>0\}\times(0,T)\]
    for an arbitrary $x^0\in\R^n$, then as is shown 
    in \cite{L1} Chapter 1 corollary 5.1, the uniqueness theorem holds, and
    \[F=H_0^1(\Omega) \times L^2(\Omega),\quad
    F'=H^{-1}(\Omega) \times L^2(\Omega).\]
    Hence the problem is completely solved in this case.
\end{itemize}

